messages:
ashley_cs: Hey team — onboarding for ScaleQ hit intermittent failures during SDK setup. Customer sees WS auth succeed then 429s after ~30s, sometimes followed by a 401 when they rotate tokens. Logs attached below.

raj_dev: Can you paste the minimal log snippet? and the SDK version?

ashley_cs: SDK v1.4.2 (browser client). Log:```
[2026-03-22T14:02:11Z] ws-open OK id=conn-1 auth=200
[2026-03-22T14:02:43Z] ws-msg send payload=init user=acct123
[2026-03-22T14:02:44Z] http 429 GET /v1/clients/preconnect (x-ratelimit-remaining=0 retry-after=18)
[2026-03-22T14:02:46Z] ws-close id=conn-1 reason=rate_limited
[2026-03-22T14:02:51Z] ws-open id=conn-2 auth=401 token_expired
```

olivia_sre: Interesting — looks like they're doing a burst of concurrent preconnects. The 429 is from our preconnect endpoint not the websocket handshake. Are they opening per-tab/per-worker connections?

ashley_cs: Customer says their SPA spawns a background worker per document tab and each worker does a connect on load. So ~12 connections at once during onboarding.

raj_dev: That will trigger the preconnect throttle. Recommended pattern: reuse a single shared connection per user session or limit concurrent preconnects to <= 4 with jitter. Also have the client honor `Retry-After`. Example snippet:```js
// keep a shared connection pool (max 4)
const pool = new Map();
async function getConn(userId){
  if (pool.has(userId)) return pool.get(userId);
  // backoff + jitter on initial connect failures
  await jitteredBackoff();
  const conn = await createWs(userId);
  pool.set(userId, conn);
  return conn;
}
```

mike_support: Also confirm header usage — token should be passed as `Authorization: Bearer <token>` in WS subprotocol handshake or initial HTTP preconnect. If they rotate tokens mid-session, ensure they trigger a controlled reconnect rather than mass reconnect.

ashley_cs: I shared that guidance. They asked about heartbeat/keepalive because they saw connections get torn after 30s. Any recommended heartbeat interval?

olivia_sre: Heartbeat 30s is fine, but avoid aligning across clients. Add a small random offset so not everyone pings at the exact second. If connections drop after the 429, the pool tries to reconnect — add exponential backoff and cap concurrent recon attempts.

raj_dev: Quick checklist I sent them: [ ] consolidate per-user connects, [ ] respect X-RateLimit and Retry-After, [ ] add jittered backoff, [ ] reuse token across short-lived reconnects or drain old token gracefully, [ ] limit pool to 4.

ashley_cs: ci-bot:quota-check ran: "preconnect-burst pattern detected for acct123 — peak concurrent preconnects=12 — recommend client-side pooling."

mike_support: One more edge: if they use an edge proxy (NGINX) with http2 connection multiplexing, verify header size and cookie duplication — oversized headers can cause auth fallback. Ask them to send `curl -I` of the preconnect call to confirm.

ashley_cs: Customer will try the pool change + jittered backoff and report back. I'll follow up in 24h. Thanks everyone :thumbsup:
